1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:03,608
Speaker 2: The deeper cut came from his
marriage to Loni Anderson, a wedding

2
00:00:03,618 --> 00:00:07,248
built with enough spectacle that a custom
stagecoach was part of the gift list.

3
00:00:07,708 --> 00:00:12,097
A horse-drawn carriage built from scratch
and stamped with his own ranch name, as if

4
00:00:12,097 --> 00:00:15,857
two people getting married needed a piece
of frontier theater to prove it was real.

5
00:00:16,787 --> 00:00:21,157
When it ended in 1993, the divorce
did not just cost him a settlement.

6
00:00:21,767 --> 00:00:24,498
During the proceedings, he was
paying close to two hundred and fifty

7
00:00:24,498 --> 00:00:27,767
thousand dollars a month just to stay
married to a woman he was already

8
00:00:27,767 --> 00:00:31,358
losing before it finally closed with
a two million dollar payment and a

9
00:00:31,358 --> 00:00:33,207
vacation home handed over on top.

10
00:00:34,028 --> 00:00:37,247
That is the reason that ballroom
in Las Vegas existed at all.

11
00:00:38,188 --> 00:00:42,487
By 2014, with the bankruptcy long
behind him but the bleeding never fully

12
00:00:42,488 --> 00:00:46,597
stopped, Burt Reynolds sat down with
an auction house and sold the physical

13
00:00:46,598 --> 00:00:48,268
proof of the life he used to have.

14
00:00:48,837 --> 00:00:53,087
The horse and rider statue, the
wedding stagecoach, piece by piece

15
00:00:53,438 --> 00:00:57,218
to strangers with paddles just
to stay solvent one more year.

16
00:00:58,017 --> 00:01:00,458
But money was never the
thing he actually regretted.

17
00:01:01,287 --> 00:01:03,617
Ask him, and he will tell
you a different name.

18
00:01:04,357 --> 00:01:08,287
In his own memoir, published two
years before he died, Burt Reynolds

19
00:01:08,287 --> 00:01:11,378
did not spend his final chapter
on the bankruptcy or the auction.

20
00:01:12,188 --> 00:01:16,128
He spent it on Sally Field, the
co-star from Smokey and the Bandit,

21
00:01:16,527 --> 00:01:20,437
the woman he was with before Loni
Anderson, the one he called, in his

22
00:01:20,438 --> 00:01:22,488
own words, "The one that got away."

23
00:01:23,127 --> 00:01:26,917
He did not blame the industry or
bad luck or a network that canceled

24
00:01:26,917 --> 00:01:28,477
a show one season too early.

25
00:01:29,207 --> 00:01:30,317
He blamed himself.

26
00:01:30,957 --> 00:01:34,167
"I was just an arrogant jerk,"
he said, "and she told me

27
00:01:34,167 --> 00:01:35,687
exactly that at the time."

28
00:01:36,357 --> 00:01:40,697
He spent decades as the most wanted man
in Hollywood and considered the biggest

29
00:01:40,707 --> 00:01:44,257
loss of his life to be a relationship he
talked himself out of in his thirties.

30
00:01:45,008 --> 00:01:47,177
And here is the turn nobody sees coming.

31
00:01:47,677 --> 00:01:52,167
The same stubbornness that cost him Sally
Field almost cost him his last great role.

32
00:01:53,067 --> 00:01:57,527
In the late 1990s, a young director
named Paul Thomas Anderson wanted

33
00:01:57,537 --> 00:02:01,307
Burt Reynolds for a film about the
adult film industry in the 1970s,

34
00:02:01,648 --> 00:02:03,817
playing a director not unlike himself.

35
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:05,677
Reynolds said no.

36
00:02:06,247 --> 00:02:07,997
He said no seven times.

37
00:02:08,507 --> 00:02:12,257
He called the material uncomfortable,
said it was not his kind of film,

38
00:02:12,567 --> 00:02:14,307
and meant it every time he said it.

39
00:02:15,147 --> 00:02:18,507
Paul Thomas Anderson did not argue
about the money or the prestige.

40
00:02:18,997 --> 00:02:24,147
He told him one sentence: "Play it exactly
like this, and it will win you an award."

41
00:02:24,967 --> 00:02:26,197
Reynolds finally agreed.

42
00:02:27,147 --> 00:02:30,127
The film was Boogie Nights, and
the performance earned him his

43
00:02:30,128 --> 00:02:34,007
only Academy Award nomination at
sixty-one years old, playing a

44
00:02:34,007 --> 00:02:37,227
version of the ambition he understood
better than anyone in that room.

45
00:02:37,977 --> 00:02:39,478
It should have been the second act.

46
00:02:39,878 --> 00:02:40,587
It was not.

47
00:02:41,227 --> 00:02:44,117
The years after Boogie
Nights were not a rebuilding.

48
00:02:44,378 --> 00:02:46,517
They were a slow, quiet subtraction.

49
00:02:47,048 --> 00:02:49,507
A quintuple heart bypass in 2010.

50
00:02:50,207 --> 00:02:52,507
Smaller parts, smaller paychecks.

51
00:02:52,768 --> 00:02:56,007
The syndication money from
decades earlier, long since spent.

52
00:02:57,098 --> 00:03:01,748
On September 6th, 2018, Burt Reynolds
suffered a heart attack in a Florida

53
00:03:01,758 --> 00:03:04,088
hospital and died at eighty-two years old.

54
00:03:05,068 --> 00:03:08,368
His niece said the family knew
he had been unwell for years, and

55
00:03:08,368 --> 00:03:10,638
still, somehow, this was unexpected.

56
00:03:11,418 --> 00:03:14,768
By the end, he weighed under one
hundred pounds and could not walk into

57
00:03:14,768 --> 00:03:16,238
a room without someone holding him up.

58
00:03:16,757 --> 00:03:19,827
The same body that once outran
police cars on camera for a living.

59
00:03:20,538 --> 00:03:23,337
The man who was the most wanted
person in America for five straight

60
00:03:23,347 --> 00:03:25,577
years ended up needing help to stand.

61
00:03:26,417 --> 00:03:30,317
Think back to that hospital bed
in 1957, the one that was supposed

62
00:03:30,317 --> 00:03:31,927
to end his story before it began.

63
00:03:32,957 --> 00:03:36,478
Nobody in that room could have told you
it would lead to Deliverance or Smokey

64
00:03:36,488 --> 00:03:41,177
and the Bandit or a Las Vegas ballroom
or an Oscar nomination he almost refused.

65
00:03:41,917 --> 00:03:44,218
That is the part a
highlight reel leaves out.

66
00:03:44,797 --> 00:03:48,037
The same refusal to accept a closed
door that turned a ruined football

67
00:03:48,038 --> 00:03:50,938
career into an acting career
was never a switch he could turn

68
00:03:50,938 --> 00:03:52,428
off once it had made him famous.

69
00:03:53,117 --> 00:03:54,497
It does not ask permission.

70
00:03:55,257 --> 00:03:58,447
It cannot tell the difference between a
knee injury worth fighting through and a

71
00:03:58,448 --> 00:04:00,217
restaurant chain worth walking away from.

72
00:04:01,148 --> 00:04:05,007
So the real question was never how Burt
Reynolds lost sixty million dollars.

73
00:04:05,688 --> 00:04:06,948
Anyone can lose money.

74
00:04:07,608 --> 00:04:10,447
The real question is what
it costs a person to be that

75
00:04:10,447 --> 00:04:12,007
certain of himself for that long.

76
00:04:12,538 --> 00:04:14,297
Certain enough to bet on restaurants.

77
00:04:15,038 --> 00:04:17,937
Certain enough to borrow against
a show that had not sold yet.

78
00:04:18,727 --> 00:04:21,397
Certain enough to walk away
from the one person he later

79
00:04:21,398 --> 00:04:22,987
admitted he actually loved.

80
00:04:23,917 --> 00:04:26,807
Burt Reynolds spent his entire
career playing men who never

81
00:04:26,807 --> 00:04:28,448
doubted themselves for a second.

82
00:04:29,347 --> 00:04:31,437
It turns out that was never a performance.

83
00:04:32,218 --> 00:04:35,797
It was the one role he could not put down
when the cameras stopped rolling, and

84
00:04:35,797 --> 00:04:38,897
it is the only one that ever actually
cost him something he could not buy

85
00:04:38,907 --> 00:04:43,157
back with a horse and rider statue or a
settlement check or an Oscar nomination

86
00:04:43,157 --> 00:04:46,047
arriving twenty years too late to change
the ending he had already written for

87
00:04:46,047 --> 00:04:48,578
himself back in that hospital bed in 1957.
